slots.reasoning:
(a) Time left: about 36 days, until the window closes on December 31, 2026. The window opened November 22 and no qualifying contact has been reported yet.

(b) Status quo: No so far. The Shenzhen meeting on November 19 fell just before the window, so a new call or meeting is needed.

(c) No scenario: Having just met in Shenzhen with little result, neither leader sees a need to call soon. Putin sends Lavrov or another official to the Miami G20, the peace talks stay at envoy level (Witkoff and Kushner), and the next call slips into January, consistent with the 2–3 month gaps seen earlier in 2026.

(d) Yes scenario: Another envoy round or a US push for a deal before or at the G20 prompts a Trump-Putin call, as happened in September and October. Alternatively, Putin attends the G20 at Doral on December 14–15 and they meet, or they hold a holiday or year-end call like the one I recall from late December 2025.

Rationale: Using the whole of 2026, there were about six known contacts in roughly 10.5 months, which implies around a 50% chance of at least one contact in a 36-day window. Using only the pace since September (roughly monthly), the chance rises to about 70%. Several factors push toward the higher end:
- the US peace push is active and calls have tended to follow envoy trips;
- the G20 invitation is open, though I put Putin's attendance at only about 15%;
- Trump's tone toward Putin is warm;
- a single brief call is enough to resolve Yes.

Factors pulling down:
- the leaders met days ago, which reduces the immediate need for a call;
- the Kremlin's position appears to have hardened after Shenzhen;
- Putin is lukewarm on travelling to Miami;
- gaps of more than a month have occurred this year.

Balancing these, and giving some weight to the status quo, I land slightly above even.

Probability: 60%
